Current wide-field X-ray telescopes face challenges in achieving high sensitivities. To overcome this, we introduced a novel direction-findable optics system using lobster-eye structures in the context of low-energy high-energy physics for particle identification \cite{cavallari14,eo}. This design enables a high gain in acceptance, potentially increasing it by two orders of magnitude for specific applications. Leveraging this advantage, we propose building a large-area, low-cost detector array equipped with simple flat detectors, as illustrated in Figure 1. This approach could facilitate continuous, high-cadence monitoring of binary systems, galactic centers, and other variable or erupting astronomical sources. We describe the fundamental principles of this multiplexed architecture and conduct a basic feasibility study regarding detection capabilities. The concept aligns well with the scientific goals of current small X-ray telescopes like NEOSSat\footnote{http://nss.ca/project/neossat/} and IMX\footnote{http://iox.ed.ac.uk/immix/}, as well as the upcoming Indian mission NATS\footnote{http://nats.iisc.ernet.in/}. We outline the specific requirements necessary to realize this multiplexed configuration with a sensitivity matching that of contemporary wide-field X-ray telescopes.

